  "notes": "Scenario 3 — Be your teammate's moving cover\nWarrior/Hunter (Team A) versus Rogue (Team B)\n\nTHE CHALLENGE\nTeam A has Warrior at 81 HP and Hunter at 1 HP; Team B has Rogue at 100 HP. HP means remaining health. Scores are tied 19–19, with 20 needed to win. Ten ticks remain, from step 290 to 300, on the approved six-wall battlefield. Waves occur every five ticks: if the match continues, initially dead slots revive after tick 5 and can make decisions from tick 6. This is not a three-agent-only fight for the entire horizon. The next decisive kill can end the match. Use terminal Team A reward: +1 SUCCESS, 0 DRAW, −1 FAIL. A simultaneous 20–20 finish is a draw; a win on step 300 still counts.\n\nTHE FEW RULES YOU NEED\nA tick is one simultaneous decision. Each agent chooses movement and a Basic attack, an Ultimate special ability, or no combat before resolution. The three initially living agents start with Ultimate cooldown 11, so their Ultimates remain unavailable during all ten decisions. Hunter attacks from range and its Basic briefly slows the target. Warrior fights up close. Kiting means attacking while moving to keep an enemy from reaching you.\n\nAgents are physical discs of radius 0.5. They cannot simply pass through one another, and collision resolution may displace a unit that chose Stay. A body obstructs movement, not sight; standing behind Warrior does not automatically make Hunter untargetable. Warrior's nearby damage-mitigation aura is a separate effect, not the physical screen. Submitted movement is an intention; walls and other bodies determine the actual endpoint.\n\nTHE PURSUER\nChoose SharedObs and Reactive TDM BETA for Team B. SharedObs supplies authorized current teammate sightings, not hidden intentions or future actions. BETA hunts observed living enemy Priests first, Mages second and Hunters third. Initially only Warrior and Hunter are alive on Team A, so Rogue pursues Hunter even if Warrior becomes weaker. If revived Priests or Mages enter shared sight later, that priority can change its destination.\n\nThe pursued prey sets Rogue's destination; legal targets and health independently set its attack. Rogue can attack Warrior while trying to get around it to Hunter. It accepts glancing shoulder contact, so it does not need a perfectly clear route around a screen. Its estimate treats currently observed bodies as stationary; it cannot read Team A's pending moves. A moving Warrior can legitimately re-block its chosen route.\n\nTHE COORDINATED POSITIONING IDEA\nKeep Warrior between Rogue and Hunter, adjusting to the likely approach while both defenders continue attacking. An imaginary line from Rogue toward Hunter passing through Warrior's body is a useful starting picture. It is a positioning guide, not a guaranteed or universally optimal block: the Rogue can angle around a shoulder, and everyone's movement resolves together.\n\nBoth teammates maintain the screen. Warrior shifts toward the lane Rogue is likely to use; Hunter moves around Warrior's protected side instead of stepping out into that lane. Hunter must also leave Warrior room to move, and Warrior must not trap Hunter against the arena geometry. This is why Warrior acts like a moving pillar rather than a stationary shield.\n\nProtection alone is insufficient. Warrior attacks when legally in reach, and Hunter keeps ranged damage flowing while repositioning. Chasing damage can expose the 1-HP Hunter, but simply fleeing can run out the clock. These are intended tactical principles, not a newly verified fixed winning action sequence.\n\nWHAT THIS SCENARIO IS DESIGNED TO TEST\n- Teammate Modeling: anticipate where the partner needs protection, movement space and attack access.\n- Opponent Modeling: anticipate the Hunter-pursuing Rogue's shoulder approach without observing its selected action.\n- Tactical Positioning (Kiting and Body Blocking): coordinate all three bodies' likely geometry, rather than optimize each defender's position independently.\n- Balancing Defensive and Offensive Play: preserve Hunter while dealing enough damage to finish before time expires.\n\nDAMAGE PRESSURE AND EVIDENCE\nFor the initial pair alone, Warrior Basic deals 8 and Hunter Basic 6: seven full-output ticks supply at most 98 damage against Rogue's 100 HP. This is a damage budget, not a verified solution. Revived agents can move from tick 6, but their Spawn Shield blocks combat and aura participation through ticks 6–8. From tick 9 they may fight or support if their exact masks, range and sight allow it; do not treat the entire horizon as an isolated three-agent duel.\n\nThe author previously reported wins at steps 298, 299 and 300 against the retired standalone Scenario 3 controller. Those remain historical observations. The author reviewed and accepted r24. That approval completes the scenario-design gate; it is not a claim of a newly reproduced Codex winning sequence against BETA v4. A separate, older r9 test layout verifies three-tick pursuit around stationary or moving defenders; it does not establish a solution here.\n\nDevelopment limitation: the central room is sealed from the outside spawn pads; this already existed in r20, before the latest decorations. An ordinary entry route has not been established. This accepted diagnostic starts from the authored snapshot. Numerical consistency is not proof of full-state reachability from an ordinary reset; no additional reachability or design-approval gate is required for this approved scenario.\n\nThis scenario is intended for matched behavioral ablations under frozen conditions, not universal proof of a policy's internal reasoning or an Elo score. Official evaluation material must not guide training, tuning or checkpoint selection.\n\nHOW TO PLAY\nLoad the saved scenario, keep Team A Manual, choose SharedObs, and select Reactive TDM BETA for Team B. Loading an asset never selects its controller automatically. Reset repeats the loaded snapshot, not a later saved revision. If play continues, initially dead agents revive after tick 5 and become eligible for decisions from tick 6; exact masks and spawn shields still restrict actions.\n\nCurrent source: scenario_3 r25, a prose-only successor to the reviewed and accepted r24. All r24 physical content is preserved. The ten-transition horizon deliberately tests sustained body blocking; five-tick respawn waves remain canonical. Scenario 3 is the sole horizon exception in the approved eight-scenario suite. BETA is internally scenario_5 behavior v4. Without an observed living Priest, Mage or Hunter, Rogue movement falls back to ALPHA: nearest observed enemy, otherwise map center. Its independent Basic-radius combat rule remains. Non-Rogues use ALPHA rules."
}
